\begin{frontmatter}

\title{VMAF-Aware Runtime Shielding for Constrained\\ Deep Reinforcement Learning in Adaptive Video Streaming:\\ A Strictly Pareto-Improving Projection}

\author[uta]{Saeed Zarbi}
\author[uta]{Leili Farzinvash}
\author[uta]{Pedram Salehpour\corref{c1}}
\cortext[c1]{Corresponding author}
\ead[c1]{psalehpour@tabrizu.ac.ir}

\address[uta]{Department of Computer Engineering, Faculty of Electrical and Computer Engineering, University of Tabriz, Tabriz, Iran}

\begin{abstract}
Client-side Adaptive Bitrate (ABR) control must balance perceptual quality against playback-stall risk, yet purely reward-maximizing deep reinforcement learning (DRL) often learns aggressive bitrate choices that inflate tail stall risk under throughput volatility, while always-on safety projections layered on top of such policies trade away visual quality in opaque, non-tunable ways.
This paper revisits DASH/HLS bitrate selection under a constrained Markov Decision Process (CMDP) viewpoint: maximize expected visual quality subject to empirical budgets on rebuffer accumulation and quality switching (\emph{smoothness}), using a primal--dual Lagrangian relaxation trained with Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO).
The training stack consumes informative client-side lookahead (near-future chunk information) and Lyapunov-style buffer-regularization cues that stabilize exploration in trace-driven rollouts.
On top of the learned policy we contribute a \textit{VMAF-aware Pareto-tunable runtime shield}: a deterministic action projection that, when the catastrophic-download test fires, selects the highest-VMAF rung satisfying a buffer-margin viability constraint subject to a small VMAF-loss budget rather than uniformly decrementing the bitrate index, and whose deployment-time \emph{catastrophic ratio} knob traces an entire operating front without retraining.
Our headline empirical finding is a \emph{strict three-way Pareto improvement}: at the recommended operating point (VMAF-loss tolerance $=$ 1.0), the proposed shield simultaneously increases mean session QoE by \VPairedDQoEvsLeg{}, reduces mean rebuffering by \VPairedDRbvsLeg\,\%, and increases mean VMAF by \VPairedDVMvsLeg{}, all paired against the standard index-decrement legacy shield on the \emph{same} policy and trace seeds (paired Wilcoxon, $n=\VNepisodes$; $p=\VPairedPQoEvsLeg$ for QoE, $p=\VPairedPRbvsLeg$ for rebuffer).
Tightening the VMAF-loss budget further to tolerance $=$ 0.8 widens the win to \VPairedDQoEvsLegStr\, paired QoE, $\VPairedDRbvsLegStr\,\%$ rebuffering, and $+\VPairedDVMvsLegStr$ VMAF, all simultaneously and statistically significant.
A controlled sweep over $\VShieldVariants$ shield variants---spanning VMAF-aware projection, $H$-step lookahead rollouts, risk-gating, and catastrophic-ratio sweeps---collapses into only $\VShieldBehaviors$ behavioral classes, surfacing the VMAF-aware fallback (rather than the threshold knob alone) as the dominant Pareto driver.
Removing the shield from a co-designed policy at inference inflates rebuffering by $|{\VPairedDRbvsOff}|\,\%$ ($p=\VPairedPRbvsOff$), confirming that the shield is operationally indispensable and that policy/shield co-design is the operationally faithful pattern. Against widely cited baselines under identical evaluation traces, the same operating point reduces mean rebuffering by \VRbReductionVsPen\,\% versus Pensieve and \VRbReductionVsMPC\,\% versus RobustMPC.
The contribution is therefore a deployable, auditable, and \emph{strictly Pareto-improving} runtime shield with a single tunable knob.
\end{abstract}

