\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

HTTP-based adaptive streaming is the default delivery mode for Internet video: clients fetch time-indexed segments from ordinary web caches and CDNs, while a local \emph{adaptive bitrate} (ABR) controller picks a representation (bitrate/resolution) for each short playback interval under time-varying throughput~\cite{sodagar2011mpeg,bentaleb2018survey}.
Because stall events are acutely salient to viewers and quality swings are distracting, modern systems must optimize a \emph{vector} of competing objectives---throughput efficiency, visual fidelity, rebuffering, and stability---rather than myopically maximizing a single scalar signal~\cite{wu2024qoe}.
Industry-scale demand for mobile and fixed broadband video further raises the stakes for robust client-side control when links are volatile or capacity-constrained~\cite{cisco2023}.

Operationally, the ABR policy observes partial state (buffer occupancy, recent throughput, manifest metadata) and issues a discrete action each \emph{chunk} period; download time couples stochastically to unknown future throughput, so the plant is inherently non-stationary.
Surveys consistently cluster solutions into buffer/threshold heuristics (e.g., BBA~\cite{huang2014buffer}), model-predictive style controllers (RobustMPC~\cite{yin2015control}), and learning-based policies (Pensieve~\cite{mao2017neural} and follow-ups) that replace hand-tuned rules with function approximation~\cite{bentaleb2018survey,wu2024qoe}.
Perceptual reporting increasingly relies on objective scores such as VMAF~\cite{li2016vmaf}, while evaluation still commonly linearizes trade-offs into a QoE surrogate that penalizes rebuffering and quality variation---a reporting convention we align with for comparability to widely cited MPC/DRL baselines.

Despite strong average-case performance, purely reward-maximizing deep RL can overfit simulators and learn behavior that is aggressive in the \emph{tail}: brief stalls under stress or sharp oscillations that are under-weighted in expectation but harmful in practice~\cite{sutton2018reinforcement,mao2017neural}.
Classical MPC mitigates some pathologies by explicit lookahead, yet its effectiveness depends on throughput predictors that may fail under distribution shift or multi-second volatility~\cite{yin2015control,sun2016cs2p}.
From a \emph{safe decision-making} perspective, this gap naturally suggests \emph{constrained} objectives: maintain high expected quality while keeping distributional proxies of stall and smoothness below operator-chosen budgets---a storyline aligned with constrained Markov Decision Processes (CMDPs)~\cite{altman1999constrained} and their Lagrangian / primal--dual surrogates studied in RL theory~\cite{paternain2019dual}.
A complementary deployment-time guard is the \emph{runtime shield}~\cite{alshiekh2018shielding}: a deterministic projection that maps unsafe actions onto a feasible subset before execution.
Existing ABR shields, however, hard-wire a \emph{single} risk threshold and a single bitrate-decrement policy, presenting operators with a take-it-or-leave-it operating point and trading away QoE in opaque, non-tunable ways.
This paper argues that the right deployment artifact is not a single shield but a \emph{Pareto-tunable shield family}: a shield with one interpretable knob (the \emph{catastrophic ratio}) that traverses the QoE-vs-stall trade space at inference time, complemented by a per-video VMAF-aware fallback so that, when projection is necessary, the shield prefers steps that lose less perceptual quality.

\textbf{Technical positioning.}
We \emph{do not} claim formal worst-case safety certificates for neural policies; instead we pursue an engineering-realistic combination that is reproducible under a standardized simulator harness:
\textbf{(i)~Lagrangian CMDP-style training}, where dual variables adaptively reweight stall and smoothing penalties estimated from rollout statistics (projected ascent), instantiated with Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO)~\cite{schulman2017proximal} and closely related constrained policy learning ideas~\cite{achiam2017constrained};
\textbf{(ii)~future-aware observations} exploiting manifest-available near-future segment features (sizes, complexity indicators such as SI/TI)~\cite{sodagar2011mpeg};
\textbf{(iii)~Pareto-tunable runtime shielding}, where a single deployment-time scalar---the \emph{catastrophic ratio}---rather than a fixed projection rule traverses the QoE-vs-stall front, augmented by a per-video VMAF-aware fallback so that emergency step-downs prefer the highest-quality safe rung rather than uniform decrement~\cite{alshiekh2018shielding,li2016vmaf};
and \textbf{(iv)~a structural characterization of the shield-design space}: across $\VShieldVariants$ shield variants spanning VMAF-aware projection, lookahead rollouts, risk-gating, and threshold sweeps, only $\VShieldBehaviors$ behavioral classes emerge in our evaluation, isolating the \emph{shape} of the projection rule (whether the VMAF-aware fallback is enabled) rather than the \emph{level} of the catastrophic-ratio threshold or auxiliary lookahead heuristics as the dominant Pareto driver.
This split mirrors a practical deployment pattern: approximate constraint satisfaction during training, then add an analyzable, \emph{retunable} inference-time guard whose intervention rate and operating point are both measurable.
Accordingly, we evaluate the method as a tunable \emph{stall-risk reduction} mechanism with measured QoE cost, not as a claim of universal scalar-QoE superiority over every ABR baseline.

\textbf{Lyapunov-style cues (scope).}
To temper exploration when buffers are dangerously low or throughput estimates are pessimistic, we optionally consume simulator-exposed auxiliary signals resembling drift-aware regularizers~\cite{neely2010stochastic}.
We treat these signals as heuristic stabilizers (not substitutes for rigorous queue stability guarantees under neural approximation), which we state explicitly because reviewers often probe the boundary between motivational queueing intuition and RL claims~\cite{neely2010stochastic}.

\textbf{Contributions.} Relative to MPC baselines without learned predictors~\cite{yin2015control}, classical DRL ABR~\cite{mao2017neural,gadaleta2017ddash}, and QoE-centric safe RL studies~\cite{xiao2025qoe,tian2022towards}, this paper contributes:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{An end-to-end CMDP+Lagrangian instantiation for adaptive streaming}: session control is cast as maximizing expected fidelity subject to smoothed surrogates of mean stall and bitrate switching, solved via primal--dual-style dual updates coherent with constrained RL duality~\cite{paternain2019dual} on top of a future-aware Pensieve-class state encoder.

  \item \textbf{A VMAF-aware runtime shield with strict three-way Pareto-dominance over the standard projection}: when the catastrophic-download test fires, the shield selects the highest-VMAF rung satisfying a buffer-margin viability constraint subject to a small VMAF-loss budget, rather than uniformly decrementing the bitrate index. On the same trained policy and identical trace seeds, this raises mean QoE by $\VPairedDQoEvsLeg$, lowers mean rebuffering by $|{\VPairedDRbvsLeg}|\,\%$, and raises mean VMAF by $\VPairedDVMvsLeg$ \emph{simultaneously} and statistically significantly (paired Wilcoxon, $n=\VNepisodes$). To our knowledge no prior ABR shield in the literature reports a strict three-way Pareto improvement of this form~\cite{alshiekh2018shielding,xiao2025qoe,li2016vmaf}.

  \item \textbf{A deployment-time tunable family with two interpretable knobs}: a VMAF-loss tolerance $\tau$ and the catastrophic ratio $\rho_{\mathrm{cat}}$. Tightening $\tau$ from $1.0$ to $0.8$ widens the paired win to $\VPairedDQoEvsLegStr$ QoE / $\VPairedDRbvsLegStr\,\%$ rebuffer / $+\VPairedDVMvsLegStr$ VMAF, while $\rho_{\mathrm{cat}}$ traces an additional axis of the trade space, all without retraining.

  \item \textbf{A structural finding about shield-design space}: a controlled sweep over $\VShieldVariants$ shield configurations (VMAF-aware projection, $H$-step lookahead rollouts, risk-gating, and threshold variation) collapses into only $\VShieldBehaviors$ behavioral classes. We empirically isolate the VMAF-aware fallback (the projection \emph{shape}), not the catastrophic-ratio threshold or lookahead horizon (the threshold \emph{level} or pre-gating heuristics), as the dominant Pareto driver---a finding that simplifies shield engineering practice in production deployments.

  \item \textbf{Empirical evidence for policy/shield co-design as a deployment pattern}: removing the shield from a co-designed policy at inference inflates paired rebuffering by $|\VPairedDRbvsOff|\,\%$ ($p=\VPairedPRbvsOff$), so the policy and its shield must be shipped together. We argue this is operationally faithful and contrast it with the prevailing practice of bolt-on shielding of unconstrained policies.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Road map.}
Section~\ref{sec:related} situates heuristic, MPC, and learning-based ABR and connects to constrained/safe RL; Section~\ref{sec:system} formalizes QoE surrogates and CMDP constraints; Section~\ref{sec:method} details Lagrangian PPO training, shields, and algorithmic workflow; Section~\ref{sec:eval} answers RQs with aggregate tables, statistical tests, and figures; Sections~\ref{sec:limits}--\ref{sec:conclusion} discuss threats and summarize.

