\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

We organize prior art along axes that mirror our design choices: classical QoE-centric control, MPC-style lookahead, purely learned bitrate policies, constrained/safe RL, and orthogonal system-level streaming research (edge-assisted delivery, QoE proxies). Table~\ref{tab:comparison} then condenses representative paradigms and positions our instantiation.

\subsection{HTTP adaptive streaming \& QoE-oriented control}

MPEG-DASH and similar standards decouple packaging from transport: the client obtains a ladder of representations plus chunk timing and nominally runs a bitrate policy purely from manifest-accessible cues and observable downloads~\cite{sodagar2011mpeg}.
Surveys document a large design space spanning buffer rules, predictor-assisted MPC, and learning~\cite{bentaleb2018survey,wu2024qoe}.
QoE surrogates typically trade average perceptual fidelity (bitrate or VMAF~\cite{li2016vmaf}) against stalls and instability; this multi-objective viewpoint is foundational for both classical and learning-based controllers~\cite{wu2024qoe}.

\textbf{Heuristic \& optimization-flavored controllers.}
Buffer-based policies such as BBA~\cite{huang2014buffer} demonstrate that simple occupancy rules can be surprisingly effective at scale, but they do not explicitly encode fine-grained horizon optimization.
BOLA reformulates ABR as an online optimization problem with provable structure under a Lyapunov-style stability argument for buffer evolution~\cite{spiteri2018bola}: this is spiritually related to drift-based safe RL~\cite{chow2018lyapunov} but instantiated with model-specific assumptions distinct from neural CMDP+PPO wrappers.
\textbf{Throughput prediction \& parameter adaptation.}
Throughput predictors underpin many MPC and hybrid designs; CS2P~\cite{sun2016cs2p} highlights data-driven forecasting as first-class coupling to bitrate selection quality.
\textbf{Systems-level orthogonal threads.}
Systems work on edge-assisted delivery~\cite{mehrabi2019edge,kim2023http,kang2023adaptive} and parameter auto-tuning (Oboe~\cite{akhtar2018oboe}) addresses \emph{where} bitrate decisions execute or \emph{how} knobs are calibrated, complementary to declaring explicit stall/smooth budgets on a neural policy itself.

\subsection{Model-predictive ABR \& lookahead rationale}

RobustMPC~\cite{yin2015control} embodies the dominant MPC narrative in ABR: solve a constrained short-horizon program under an estimated throughput trajectory.
When predictors track reality, MPC yields interpretable trade-offs; when they do not, conservative or aggressive mis-selection can appear---especially under bursty mobile links~\cite{kvalbein2014,sun2016cs2p}.
\textbf{Hybrid control--learning.}
Recent hybrid pipelines combine differentiable or structured predictors with learning~\cite{liu2023hybrid}; our framing differs by centering primal--dual constraint handling on rollout statistics~\cite{paternain2019dual} while retaining manifest lookahead features similar in spirit (though not identical in mechanism) to MPC horizon inputs~\cite{yin2015control}.

\subsection{Deep reinforcement learning for ABR}

Pensieve~\cite{mao2017neural} popularized RL on trace-driven emulation with recurrent throughput history and QoE-motivated rewards, catalyzing a family of successors (e.g., D-DASH contemporaneous deep RL formulations~\cite{gadaleta2017ddash}).
\textbf{Strengths.}
RL naturally consumes high-dimensional histories and heterogeneous manifest metadata without brittle hand-engineered surrogates for every impairment~\cite{sutton2018reinforcement}.
\textbf{Tensions for multimedia reviewers.}
Pure scalar rewards make stall and smoothness \emph{implicit}; multi-objective trade-offs then depend on reward scaling and simulator misspecification.
Field evidence (Fugu / ``Learning in Situ'') stresses deployment gaps between offline traces and live networks~\cite{yan2019learning}, motivating transparent reporting alongside average QoE.
These observations motivate declarative surrogates for stall/smooth budgets at training time~\cite{xiao2025qoe}---distinct from swapping in a heavier reward weight by hand~\cite{bentaleb2018survey}.

\subsection{Constrained, risk-aware, \& shielded RL}

CMDPs formulate expectations with auxiliary budget constraints~\cite{altman1999constrained}; primal--dual and Lagrangian relaxations underpin practical RL with constraint feedback~\cite{paternain2019dual}.
Policy-search analogues tighten trust regions under constraint surrogates~\cite{achiam2017constrained}.
\textbf{Safety narratives.}
Tutorial-style taxonomies categorize constraint satisfaction, pessimism, shields, Lyapunov methods, and more~\cite{tian2022towards}.
Lyapunov-style penalties appear as principled scaffolding in some safe RL recipes~\cite{chow2018lyapunov}; multimedia-specific treatments increasingly adopt CMDP viewpoints for QoE-sensitive streaming~\cite{xiao2025qoe}.

\textbf{Shields.}
Logical/runtime shielding prunes or repairs actions before execution to respect specifications~\cite{alshiekh2018shielding}.
For streaming, shields can be viewed as lightweight \emph{inference-time} stabilizers whose intervention frequency is measurable---a deployment-friendly complement to internal reward shaping when tail stall must be reduced without discarding the learned policy wholesale.

\subsection{Positioning of this work}

Relative to classical MPC~\cite{yin2015control}, we replace hand-rolled horizon optimization with a learned policy but retain explicit constraint signaling through Lagrangian duals~\cite{paternain2019dual} and manifest lookahead comparable to client-visible metadata~\cite{sodagar2011mpeg}.
Relative to Pensieve-style DRL~\cite{mao2017neural,gadaleta2017ddash}, we externalize stall/smoothness \emph{budgets} rather than burying them inside a single tuned scalar return.
Relative to QoE-aware safe RL works in streaming~\cite{xiao2025qoe}, we emphasize a reproducible split: dual updates during training plus deterministic shields at inference with logged intervention rates~\cite{alshiekh2018shielding}, aligning evaluation with both QoE and stall/switch summaries~\cite{wu2024qoe}.
We deliberately do \emph{not} blur these contributions with orthogonal systems optimizations (edge assistance~\cite{kang2023adaptive,kim2023http}, knob auto-tuning~\cite{akhtar2018oboe}) although such layers could stack atop our controllers in future deployments.

