\section{System Model and Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:system}

\subsection{Client-Side Abstraction}
We adopt the standard chunked HTTP adaptive-streaming abstraction~\cite{yin2015control,mao2017neural,sodagar2011mpeg}: representations on a bitrate ladder \(\mathcal{B}=\{1,\ldots,L\}\) index chunk sizes signaled in the manifest; the client observes partial state \(s_k\) at chunk boundary \(k\), commits an action \(a_k\in\mathcal{B}\), then downloads encoded media for horizon chunk \(k{+}1\) under an unknown time-varying channel.
Decisions evolve on the \emph{chunk} clock (typical playback duration \(T_{\mathrm{c}}\), e.g., \(T_{\mathrm{c}}{=}4\,\mathrm{s}\) in our emulation), aligning controller design with MPC/DRL benchmarks in the Pensieve lineage~\cite{mao2017neural,yin2015control}.

\subsection{Chunk-Timescale Plant Dynamics}

\subsubsection{Throughput and Latency}
Let \(\hat{C}_k>0\) denote the effective empirical throughput allocated to chunk \(k{+}1\) downloads (matching the estimator used by emulation/baselines to keep comparisons apples-to-apples).
Choosing representation \(a_k\) induces a chunk payload size \(\sigma_{k}(a_k)\) inferred from ladder metadata; with throughput clamped \(\tilde{C}_k=\max\{\hat{C}_k,\epsilon\}\) for a tiny \(\epsilon{>}0\) to avoid division blow-ups~\cite{yin2015control}, nominal download seconds are \(d_k=\sigma_{k}(a_k)/\tilde{C}_k\).

\subsubsection{Buffer and Stall}
Let \(B_k\ge 0\) be buffer occupancy at the start of download \(k\), measured in \emph{seconds of media consumed at nominal rate}~\cite{yin2015control,mao2017neural}.
While fetching chunk \(k{+}1\), stall seconds accumulate when playback cannot consume media faster than arrivals:
\begin{equation}
    \tau^{\mathrm{stall}}_k=\max\{0,\, d_k-B_k\}.
\end{equation}
After the \(k^{\text{th}}\) interaction completes under this abstracted fluid model~\cite{yin2015control}, nominal buffer evolves as
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:buffer}
    B_{k+1}=\max\{0,B_k-d_k\}+T_{\mathrm{c}},
\end{equation}
i.e., buffer drains by download time (stall captured above) and refills by one chunk of playback credit when the next segment is ready.
We intentionally keep \(B_k\) in seconds because it aligns with QoE surrogates (stall vs.\ bitrate trade-offs~\cite{bentaleb2018survey}) and MPC-style constraints~\cite{yin2015control}.

\subsubsection{Observation--Action MDP Skeleton}
Transitions are stochastic through \(\hat{C}_k\) sampled from curated traces~\cite{bentaleb2018survey,fcc2015mba,kvalbein2014}; deterministic given \((s_k,a_k,\hat{C}_k)\) under \(\sigma_{k}\) and buffering rules~\eqref{eq:buffer}.
Formal CMDP formulations treat constraint costs as additional signals on transitions~\cite{altman1999constrained}; our implementation aggregates them per episode for dual updates~\cite{paternain2019dual}.

\subsection{Evaluation QoE Surrogate}

\textbf{Separating benchmarking from optimization.}
Controlled experiments still report a \emph{scalar per-chunk QoE surrogate} analogous to RobustMPC/Pensieve disclosures~\cite{yin2015control,mao2017neural}:
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{QoE}^{\mathrm{(rep)}}_{k} = q_k - \alpha\, \mathrm{smooth}_k - \beta\, \tau^{\mathrm{stall}}_k,
\end{equation}
where \(q_k\) denotes instantaneous fidelity (VMAF here~\cite{li2016vmaf}), \(\mathrm{smooth}_k=| q_k-q_{k-1}| \) proxies quality switching nuisance, \(\alpha,\beta\) match simulator coefficients used by baselines, and \(\mathrm{smooth}_k\) adopts boundary conventions consistent with their shared evaluation harness.
Tables and figures aggregate \(\sum_{k\in\mathrm{ep}}\mathrm{QoE}^{\mathrm{(rep)}}_{k}\) (``session QoE'') to align with community practice while our \emph{optimization} objective below keeps \(q_k\) in the primal term and separates stall/smooth surrogates into constraints/costs~\cite{paternain2019dual}.

\subsection{Constrained Stochastic Control}

We aim to maximize fidelity along trajectories induced by \(\pi_\theta\) while explicitly bounding expected stall/smooth surrogates per session:
\begin{align}
\label{eq:prim}
    \max_{\pi_\theta} ~ & \mathbb{E}_{\pi_\theta,\,\rho}\Big[\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}_{e}} q_k\Big],\\
\label{eq:c1}
    \mathrm{s.t.} ~~ & \mathbb{E}_{\pi_\theta,\,\rho}\Big[\mathrm{RB}(e)\Big] \le \delta_{\mathrm{rf}},\\
\label{eq:c2}
    & \mathbb{E}_{\pi_\theta,\,\rho}\Big[\mathrm{SM}(e)\Big] \le \delta_{\mathrm{sm}},
\end{align}
indexed over stochastic episodes \(e\) drawn i.i.d.\ from \(\rho\) (videos $\times$ trace partitions). \(\mathcal{K}_{e}=\{1,\ldots,K_{e}\}\) indexes chunk spans per episode.
\textbf{Normalized rebuffer.}
Consistent with our reporting tables, let total playback-time \(T^{\mathrm{play}}_{e}=K_{e}T_{\mathrm{c}}\). Define
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{RB}(e)=\frac{1}{T^{\mathrm{play}}_{e}}\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}_{e}} \tau^{\mathrm{stall}}_k,
\end{equation}
i.e., \emph{stall fraction of session wall-clock}; scaling \(\mathrm{RB}{\times}100\) matches headline ``mean rebuffer ratio (\%)'' in Section~\ref{sec:eval}.

\textbf{Smoothness surrogate.}
We adopt the fidelity-jump analogue used during training/logging:
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{SM}(e)=\frac{1}{K_{e}}\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}_{e}} \mathrm{smooth}_k.
\end{equation}
Targets \(\delta_{\mathrm{rf}},\delta_{\mathrm{sm}}\) operationalize stakeholder preferences (``keep mean stall below \(x\%\) while limiting oscillation''); we treat them as fixed hyper-parameters for reproducibility, mirroring MPC-style QoE knobs~\cite{yin2015control}.

\textbf{Relaxation caveat.}
Equation~\eqref{eq:prim}--\eqref{eq:c2} summarizes the stochastic program motivating our Lagrangian PPO instantiation; empirical CMDP+PPO minimizes sample estimates with warm-started duals and clipped trust regions~\cite{achiam2017constrained,schulman2017proximal}, so finite-sample residuals can remain until additional regularization (Sec.~\ref{sec:method}) activates.

\subsection{Lagrangian Relaxation and Dual Ascent}

\textbf{Penalty form.}
Introducing duals \(\lambda_r,\lambda_s\ge 0\), surrogates \(\widetilde{\mathrm{RB}}_k\) and \(\widetilde{\mathrm{SM}}_k\) denote per-step penalty channels derived from instantaneous stall \(\tau^{\mathrm{stall}}_k\) and smoothness \(\mathrm{smooth}_k\) so rolling them up coincides episode-wise with~\(\mathrm{RB}(e)\) and~\(\mathrm{SM}(e)\) up to fixed normalization factors shared across baselines in the codebase:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:L}
    \mathcal{L}(\theta,\lambda) =
    \mathbb{E}_{\pi_\theta,\,\rho}\Big[\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}_{e}} \big( q_k - \lambda_r\, \widetilde{\mathrm{RB}}_k - \lambda_s\, \widetilde{\mathrm{SM}}_k \big)\Big],
\end{equation}
with \(\widetilde{\mathrm{RB}}_k\) identified with \(\tau^{\mathrm{stall}}_k\) normalization consistent with rollout logging and \(\widetilde{\mathrm{SM}}_k\) mirroring~\(\mathrm{smooth}_k\) up to clipping/scaling conventions shared with baselines.
Each training episode terminates with empirical constraint estimates \(\hat{\mathrm{RB}},\,\hat{\mathrm{SM}}\); deficits
\begin{align}
    g_r=\hat{\mathrm{RB}}-\delta_{\mathrm{rf}}, &&
    g_s=\hat{\mathrm{SM}}-\delta_{\mathrm{sm}},
\end{align}
drive projected stochastic dual ascent~\cite{paternain2019dual,altman1999constrained},
\begin{equation}
    \lambda_{\bullet}\leftarrow \Pi_{\Lambda}\big[\lambda_{\bullet}+\eta_{\bullet}\, g_{\bullet}\big],\quad \Lambda=[0,\lambda^{\max}_{r}]\times[0,\lambda^{\max}_{s}],
\end{equation}
with possibly asymmetric \((\eta_r,\eta_s)\) to prioritize stall satisfaction (mirroring operational preference for avoiding stalls over aggressive quality swings).

\textbf{Remark (auxiliary Lyapunov cues).}
Training optionally subtracts buffer-deviation and simulator Lyapunov penalty channels~\cite{neely2010stochastic} as \emph{non-constraint} stabilizers; they do not replace certificates for neural policies and are studied alongside ablations in Section~\ref{sec:eval}.

\textbf{Remark (runtime shields).}
Shields \(\mathcal{S}\) from Section~\ref{sec:method} act as deterministic maps \((s_k,a^{\mathrm{raw}}_k)\mapsto a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k\) before environment stepping; they are \emph{not} additional transition noise but explicit safety-style guards whose intervention frequency is logged for interpretability~\cite{alshiekh2018shielding}.

% =========================================
\section{Method: Lagrangian PPO and Runtime Shields}
\label{sec:method}

We realize the CMDP relaxation of Sec.~\ref{sec:system} with a \emph{primal--dual} loop compatible with large-scale policy optimization~\cite{paternain2019dual,achiam2017constrained}: PPO~\cite{schulman2017proximal} acts as the primal optimizer on a shaped Markovian reward, while episodic statistics drive projected dual ascent on \((\lambda_r,\lambda_s)\).
Runtime shields implement a separate, deterministic action map layered \emph{after} (or, optionally, around) the policy during train/eval, following the ``train then guard'' narrative common in shielded RL~\cite{alshiekh2018shielding,tian2022towards}.
All quantities share the chunk clock and symbols of Sec.~\ref{sec:system}: \(k=1,\ldots,K_e\), \(T_{\mathrm{c}}\), \(a_k\in\mathcal{B}\), \(B_k\), \(\tau^{\mathrm{stall}}_k\), buffering~\eqref{eq:buffer}, and surrogates \(\widetilde{\mathrm{RB}}_k,\widetilde{\mathrm{SM}}_k\) tied to simulator logs (\(\mathrm{VMAF}\)-based \(q_k\), instantaneous rebuffer penalties, VMAF-switch penalties).

\subsection{Observation and Policy Interface}

\textbf{Observation.}
Vector \(s_k\) concatenates three semantically isolated blocks analogous to Pensieve~\cite{mao2017neural} yet augmented with lookahead metadata advocated for DASH clients~\cite{sodagar2011mpeg}:
(i)~\emph{past throughput} represented as a bounded normalized history;
(ii)~\emph{control state}: buffer fullness, bitrate index \(a_{k-1}\), and pacing cues exposed by our emulator wrapper;
(iii)~\emph{near-future manifest features}: bitrate ladder chunk sizes plus spatial/temporal complexity tags (SI/TI) aligned with MPC-style horizon inputs~\cite{yin2015control}.
This stack remains realizable purely from manifests and client-visible measurements~\cite{sodagar2011mpeg}.

\textbf{Action.}
The policy emits a categorical distribution over finitely many bitrate indices \(a_k\in\{0,\ldots,L{-}1\}\) aligned with~\(\mathcal{B}\); shields (Sec.~\ref{sec:method:shield}) optionally remap the sampled discrete action through a deterministic projection \(\mathcal{S}_{\kappa}\) with \(\kappa\in\{\mathrm{none},\mathrm{classic},\mathrm{gate}\}\).

\subsection{Neural Backbone and PPO Primal}\label{sec:method:ppo}

Figure~\ref{fig:arch} sketches the implemented feature pipeline: normalized throughput history, buffer/last-action state, and vectorized lookahead inputs are concatenated and passed to separate Tanh MLP actor--critic heads parameterizing \(\pi_\theta(a\mid s)\) and \(V_\phi(s)\)~\cite{schulman2017proximal}.
Training adopts Stable-Baselines~3 (SB3)~\cite{raffin2021sb3,schulman2017proximal}: parallel vectorized workers collect fixed-length rollouts (\(4096\cdot 8\) transitions per optimizer step; Table~\ref{tab:hypers}), generalized advantage estimation (GAE) with \(\gamma,\lambda_{\mathrm{GAE}}\) as listed, surrogate clipping, entropy bonuses (entropy coefficient \(0.04\) for proposed agents unless ablated), decaying Adam learning rates for actor/critic heads, Tanh policy/value MLP cores (width 256 \(\times\) two layers), and optional gradient clipping~\cite{schulman2017proximal}.
Scalar-reward ABR~\cite{mao2017neural} fixes stall/smoothing trade-offs implicitly; ours injects telemetry-driven Lagrangian weights \(\lambda_r,\lambda_s\) via a reusable \texttt{gymnasium.Wrapper}~\cite{raffin2021sb3}.

