\subsection{Lagrangian Reward Wrapper}\label{sec:method:lag}

Each emulator step exposes \(\big(q_k,\texttt{rebuffer}_k,\mathrm{smooth}_k,\phi(B_k),\psi^{\mathrm{Lya}}_k\big)\): \(q_k\) is the VMAF term in~\eqref{eq:L}; \texttt{rebuffer} entries instantiate~\(\widetilde{\mathrm{RB}}_k\); \(\mathrm{smooth}_k\) feeds~\(\widetilde{\mathrm{SM}}_k\); \(\phi,\psi^{\mathrm{Lya}}\) denote buffer deviation and simulator Lyapunov penalties surfaced through \texttt{info} dictionaries with fixed coefficients \(w_b,w_\ell\) \emph{outside}~\(\lambda_{\bullet}\).

\subsubsection{Instantaneous shaped reward.}
Per chunk the wrapper composes (numerical scaling \(1/100\) matches SB3 stability conventions in our repository)
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:rtrain}
    r^{\mathrm{train}}_k
    = \frac{1}{100}\Big(
        q_k
        - \lambda_r \,\widetilde{\mathrm{RB}}_k
        - \lambda_s \,\widetilde{\mathrm{SM}}_k
        - w_b\,\phi(B_k)
        - w_\ell\,\psi^{\mathrm{Lya}}_k
    \Big),
\end{equation}
with \(\widetilde{\mathrm{RB}}_k,\widetilde{\mathrm{SM}}_k\) instantiations of the logged stall and smooth penalties (identical up to constants to the episode statistics \(\mathrm{RB}(e),\mathrm{SM}(e)\) after accumulation).

\subsubsection{Episodic dual ascent.}
At episode boundaries the wrapper maps finished rollouts to empirical constraint features mirroring Sec.~\ref{sec:system}: a mean rebuffer \emph{ratio} \(\hat{\mathrm{RB}}\) (aggregated simulator stall seconds normalized by simulated playback horizon \(K_e T_{\mathrm{c}}\)), and \(\hat{\mathrm{SM}}\) averaged per-step smooth penalty.
Compared to instantaneous \(\tau^{\mathrm{stall}}_k\) terminology in Sec.~\ref{sec:system}, \(\hat{\mathrm{RB}}\) therefore tracks the codebase ratio used for \(\lambda_r\) adaptation.
Warm-up freezes dual updates for \(H\) completed episodes (\(H{=}60\) in our V12 freeze, inherited from stabilized V9 wrapper defaults) to avoid prematurely inflating~\(\lambda_{\bullet}\) while critics still mis-estimate~\cite{sutton2018reinforcement,paternain2019dual}.
After warm-up we maintain bounded moving-average logs (window up to \(100\) recent episodes capped to \(200\) retained entries) supplying \(\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{RB}],\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{SM}]\)-style estimates for gaps \(g_r=\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{RB}]-\delta_{\mathrm{rf}}\), \(g_s=\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{SM}]-\delta_{\mathrm{sm}}\).
Updates apply \emph{asymmetric} primal-dual-inspired step sizes (\(\eta^{\pm}_r,\eta^{\pm}_s\)) emphasizing rapid inflation of \(\lambda_r\) whenever rebuffer violates \(\delta_{\mathrm{rf}}\), and gentler reductions when comfortably below target—the engineered asymmetry biases policies against tail stalls~\cite{tian2022towards}.

Both \(\lambda_r\) and \(\lambda_s\) are projected onto axis-aligned boxes \([\lambda^{\min}_{r},\lambda^{\max}_{r}] \times [\lambda^{\min}_{s},\lambda^{\max}_{s}]\) (explicit constants in artifact), preventing exploding penalties while allowing large \(\lambda^{\max}_{r}\) when aggressive streaming demands stronger stall containment.

\subsubsection{Distributed training.}
Parallel vectorized actors each host an identical wrapper; independent dual trajectories converge to mutually consistent multipliers analogous to decentralized dual ascent, while PPO minibatches amalgamate experience for stable primal updates~\cite{paternain2019dual}.

\subsection{Pareto-Tunable Runtime Shields}\label{sec:method:shield}

\textbf{Role.}
The shield \(\mathcal{S}_{\kappa,\rho_{\mathrm{cat}}}:(s_k,a^{\mathrm{raw}}_k)\mapsto(a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k,\iota_k)\) is invoked immediately before simulator stepping, yielding the executed bitrate index \(a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k\) and a binary intervention indicator \(\iota_k\in\{0,1\}\) for audit logging (Section~\ref{sec:eval}). Unlike previous ABR shields, our family exposes a \emph{single} continuous deployment knob: the catastrophic ratio \(\rho_{\mathrm{cat}}\), which traverses an entire QoE-vs-stall Pareto front without retraining.

\textbf{Inputs.}
The shield reads buffer seconds \(B_k\), current chunk index \(k\), a pessimistic throughput estimate \(\mathrm{tp\_est}=\min\{\mathrm{trace\_Mbps},\mathrm{last\_Mbps}\}\cdot\rho\) with pessimism scalar \(\rho\) (artifact default \(\rho{=}0.9\)), manifest chunk sizes \(\sigma_{k}(b)\), and the per-video VMAF ladder \(V_k(b)\) (when the VMAF-aware variant is enabled).

\textbf{Catastrophic download test.}
For each candidate bitrate index \(j\), we estimate download seconds \(d^{\mathrm{est}}_{k}(j)\) by dividing manifest-derived chunk bits by the pessimistic throughput in kbps (identical bit-matching to the simulator), then clipping to \([0,60]\)~s.

\textbf{Risk gate (\(\kappa{=}\mathrm{gate}\)).}
When \texttt{only\_when\_risky} activates, the shield leaves actions untouched whenever \(d^{\mathrm{est}}_{k}(a^{\mathrm{raw}}_k)\le (\max\{B_k,0.1\})\cdot\rho_{\mathrm{risk}}\) with \(\rho_{\mathrm{risk}}{\approx}1.1\); interventions trigger only when the estimated download dominates buffered slack by that margin.

\textbf{Pareto-tunable projection (\(\rho_{\mathrm{cat}}\) knob).}
Otherwise the shield decrements dangerously high bitrate choices when \(d^{\mathrm{est}}_{k}(a^{\mathrm{raw}}_k) > \rho_{\mathrm{cat}}\, B_k\), searching downward to the maximal index respecting \(d^{\mathrm{est}}_{k}(j)\le B_k-\mu\). The knob \(\rho_{\mathrm{cat}}\) directly controls how aggressively the shield trades QoE for safety: small \(\rho_{\mathrm{cat}}\) (e.g., $2$) intervenes early and yields the lowest rebuffering at moderate QoE cost, while larger \(\rho_{\mathrm{cat}}\) (e.g., $4$ or $5$) defers intervention, accepting slightly more rebuffering for higher QoE. Section~\ref{sec:eval} reports the empirical Pareto curve obtained by sweeping \(\rho_{\mathrm{cat}}\in\{2,3,4,5\}\); operators choose a deployment point \emph{post hoc} from this curve. An ultra-low-buffer rule (\(B_k\le 0.3\)~s) always snaps to the safest representation, regardless of \(\rho_{\mathrm{cat}}\).

\textbf{VMAF-aware fallback projection.}
Conventional shields decrement the bitrate \emph{index} uniformly when the catastrophic test fires, which is suboptimal because VMAF ladders are non-uniformly spaced: two adjacent rungs may differ by a few VMAF points, while another adjacent pair may differ by ten or more. We therefore augment the projection so that, among all rungs \(j\) satisfying the buffer-margin viability test, the shield selects \(j^\star = \arg\max_{j} V_k(j)\) subject to a small VMAF-loss budget \(\Delta V \le \tau\) relative to the policy's intended choice. When no soft-feasible rung is found we fall back to the standard light/strong projection so safety remains the primary invariant. The fallback adds at most one comparison per candidate rung at chunk granularity.

\textbf{Behavioral collapse: a structural caveat.}
A controlled sweep over $\VShieldVariants$ shield variants (Section~\ref{sec:eval:shields}) collapses into only $\VShieldBehaviors$ behavioral classes once paired against an identical policy and trace set, isolating the VMAF-aware fallback---the \emph{shape} of the projection rule---rather than the catastrophic-ratio threshold or auxiliary lookahead heuristics as the dominant Pareto driver. We exploit this by reporting the VMAF-loss tolerance $\tau$ alongside $\rho_{\mathrm{cat}}$ as the two operator-facing deployment knobs, while cataloguing the broader sweep for completeness.

\textbf{Auditing.}
All projection logic is deterministic, so intervention rates and per-rung reroute frequencies are exactly reproducible across seeds, enabling regulatory-style audit aligned with~\cite{xiao2025qoe,alshiekh2018shielding}.

\textbf{Inference-time vs.\ training-time use, and policy/shield co-design.}
Shield wrappers accompany evaluation-focused runs in the reproducibility bundle and can additionally wrap train-time transitions for \emph{shield-aware curricula}. We empirically observe (Section~\ref{sec:eval:codesign}) that policies trained \emph{with} a shield in the loop exhibit a strong dependency on shield availability at inference: removing the shield from such a policy at inference inflates rebuffering by an order of magnitude. We therefore advocate \emph{co-design} (train with shield, ship with shield) and use the catastrophic-ratio knob, rather than shield removal, as the deployment-time tunable.

\subsection{Optional Shield-Aware Shaping and Hysteresis}

\textbf{Shield-aware penalties.}
\texttt{ShieldAwarePenaltyWrapper} decreases reward by \(\beta_{\mathrm{sh}}\cdot \iota_k + \gamma_{\mathrm{sh}}|a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k-a^{\mathrm{raw}}_k|\) (\(\beta_{\mathrm{sh}}{=}0.08,\ \gamma_{\mathrm{sh}}{=}0.03\) defaults), nudging \(\pi_\theta\) to agree with shields without overwriting hard projection~\cite{alshiekh2018shielding}.

\textbf{Hysteresis.}
\texttt{HysteresisActionWrapper} caps per-step bitrate index changes \(\le\,1\) and blocks bitrate upswitches whenever \(B_k < 1.5\text{s}\), reducing jitter before shields fire.

\subsection{End-to-End Training and Deployment Algorithm}

Algorithm~\ref{alg:train} summarizes how wrappers compose with PPO; concrete hyper-parameters appear in Table~\ref{tab:hypers} and reproducibility manifests.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Lagrangian PPO Train / Shielded Evaluate}
\label{alg:train}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\STATE Instantiate vectorized SB3-PPO\((\theta)\); wrap envs with \texttt{LagrangianRewardWrapperV12} and optional hysteresis or \texttt{ShieldAwarePenalty}.
\FOR{optimization iteration until budget}
    \FOR{parallel worker \(w\)}
        \STATE Reset env; \(\lambda_{r},\lambda_{s}\) shared or replicated per-worker (artifact default).
        \FOR{environment step \(k{=}1{:}K_e\)}
            \STATE \(a^{\mathrm{raw}}_k\sim\pi_\theta(\cdot\mid s_k)\)
            \STATE \(a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k \gets \mathcal{S}_{\kappa}(s_k,a^{\mathrm{raw}}_k)\) if shield-at-train, otherwise \(a^{\mathrm{raw}}_k\).
            \STATE Step emulator with \(a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k\); log \(\{q_k,\widetilde{\mathrm{RB}}_k,\widetilde{\mathrm{SM}}_k,\phi,\psi^{\mathrm{Lya}}\}\)
            \STATE \(r^{\mathrm{train}}_k \gets\) Eq.~\eqref{eq:rtrain} plus optional shaping.
        \ENDFOR
        \STATE On episode termination: accumulate \(\hat{\mathrm{RB}},\hat{\mathrm{SM}}\); if past warm-up \(\Rightarrow\) asymmetric dual ascent with projection \(\Pi_{\Lambda}\).
        \STATE Push transitions into PPO buffer.
    \ENDFOR
    \STATE PPO.update() on minibatches (GAE + clipped surrogate + entropy losses).
\ENDFOR
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

